  "text_head": "The user has adopted my previous suggestions and added:\n1. Missing-data protocol: \"MISSING\" logged, three consecutive gaps trigger site-replacement review\n2. ≥50 m from paved roadway (exhaust heat, tire aerosol)\n3. Concrete failure: cup anemometer in chimney wake under-reading by 20-30% at low speeds\n4. Mast must clear obstructions by ≥2× its height in all azimuths (same horizon-sketch verification)\n5. Weekly report carries per-site data-completeness percentage\n6. Sites below 62% completeness flagged \"provisional\"\n\nNow I need to respond to my partner, advance a concrete design choice, and examine a possible failure with an example. Three or four concise sentences. No tools.\n\nI should:\n- Acknowledge their additions (missing-data rule, 50 m roadway, anemometer wake, completeness percentage)\n- Advance a NEW concrete design choice (something not yet covered)\n- Examine a possible failure with a specific example\n\nWhat hasn't been addressed yet? Let me think about what's been covered:\n- Instrument calibration: temperature cross-check ±0.2°C, recalibration trigger ±0.5°C\n- Site selection: 1.5 m standoff, 360° sky view, ≥50 m from roadway, ≥2× height clearance\n- Sampling: hourly\n- Missing data: \"MISSING\" logged, 3 gaps → site-replacement review, 62% completeness threshold\n- Record keeping: bound logbook, timestamp, initials, sky-condition code, serial number\n- Comparisons: weekly against NOAA/NCDC\n- Uncertainty: ±0.5°C instrument uncertainty mentioned\n- Reporting: weekly public report, completeness percentage, \"provisional\" flag\n\nWhat's missing? Let me think...\n- Barometric pressure calibration/altitude correction?\n- Rain gauge specifics (tipping bucket vs. non-recording, self-cleaning, anti-freeze)\n- Wind direction quantization (16-point vs. 32-point compass)\n- How to handle the comparison when the reference station is at different elevation (pressure correction)\n- Unit consistency / datum issues\n- Sampling: what if it's 20:59 vs 21:00? Exact timestamp protocol\n- Light cont",
  "text_tail": " a self-cleaning wiper at 1.0 m above mown grass, and in winter a graduated-cylinder secondary read at 02:00 to capture frozen accumulation that a tipping bucket cannot resolve below its 0.2 mm threshold. The failure this guards against is straightforward: a November bough overhanging the inlet by only 15° intercepts enough light-rain droplets to shave roughly 15 % off every event, so a month of 60 mm of true precipitation is logged as ~51 mm and the neighborhood's comparison column quietly trends low against the NOAA station. I therefore require the same 360° horizon-sketch at installation for the rain inlet, with any bough within 30° of the rim annotated and a pruning or relocation note entered before the first snowfall.\"\n\nThat's four sentences. Let me check: responds to partner (accepts their additions), advances concrete design (rain gauge specification), examines failure with example (bough intercepting rain, 60→51 mm). Good. Concise enough? Let me tighten.\n</think>\n\nI accept the wake-clearance rule and the 62 % completeness flag without modification; they keep the weekly bulletin honest without forcing us to discard a season of data. For the gauge variable you have left open, each site will carry a 20-inch standard rain gauge with a self-cleaning wiper at 1.0 m above mown grass, supplemented in winter by a graduated-cylinder secondary read at 02:00 to capture frozen accumulation that no tipping-bucket threshold can resolve. The failure this guards against is unglamorous but persistent: a single evergreen bough overhanging the inlet by only 15° intercepts enough light-rain droplets to shave roughly 15 % off every event, so a true monthly total of 60 mm is logged as ≈51 mm and the neighborhood's comparison column quietly trends low against the NOAA station. I therefore require the same 360° horizon-sketch at installation for the rain inlet, with any bough within 30° of the rim annotated and a pruning-or-relocation note entered before the first forecast snowfall.",
